papers.abstract:
Let $X$ be a smooth connected complex projective variety. We give a structure theorem for irreducible components of the reduced rank-two cohomology jump loci on $X$. More precisely, for such a component $Z$, if the general point of $Z$ is projectively virtually abelian or projectively rigid, then either $Z$ is a symmetric square of an algebraic torus, or a quotient of a torus by an involution, or its normalization $Z^{
u}$ is a torus. If the general point is neither, then either $Z^{
u}$ is a torus, or an '{e}tale quotient of $Q^{
u} 	imes T$ where $Q$ is a fixed-determinant, fixed-inertia component of the character variety of some DM-curve.

We study the mean estimation of Gaussian location model with star-shaped constraints under adversarial contamination, where at most $ k<N/2 $ among $ N $ observations are corrupted by an oracle adversary. We characterize the minimax squared $ \ell_2 $ risk throughout the full range of contamination level $ \varepsilon = k/N \in [0,1/2) $. The minimax risk is
  \begin{equation*}
  \mathfrak{M}\asymp \big( \delta _\varepsilon ^{*2} \vee \sigma ^2\varepsilon ^2\log \frac{e}{1-2\varepsilon } \big) \wedge d^2 ,
  \end{equation*}
  where $ \delta _\varepsilon ^* := \sup \big\{ r \geq 0: (1-2\varepsilon )\frac{Nr ^2}{\sigma ^2} \leq \log M^\mathrm{ loc }_K(r ,c) \big\} $, $ \log M^\mathrm{ loc }_K(r ,c) $ is the local entropy of constraint set $ K $, $ d $ is the diameter of $ K $ (set $ d=\infty $ if $ K $ is unbounded), $ \sigma ^2 I_n $ is the covariance of the Gaussian noise, and $ c $ is a sufficiently large constant. Our result sheds light on the interaction between contamination error and information theoretic complexity in the regime of proximity to the breakdown point $ \varepsilon \uparrow 1/2 $.

In this paper, we study once-reinforced random walk on $\mathbb{Z}$, and prove sharp Chung-type and Hartman-Wintner laws of the iterated logarithm for the range.
  Let $R_n$ denote the range at time $n$ and let $c>0$ be the reinforcement parameter. Then, almost surely, \begin{equation*} \liminf_{n\to\infty}\frac{R_n}{\sqrt{n/\log\log n}}=\frac{\pi}{\sqrt{2}}, \qquad \limsup_{n\to\infty}\frac{cR_n}{\sqrt{2n\log\log n}}=1. \end{equation*}
  The proofs are based on the independent increment decomposition of the inverse range and sharp generating function estimates.

Let $Z_F(t):=F(1/2+it) H(1/2+it)^{-1/2}$ denote Hardy's function, where the function $F(s)$ belongs to the Selberg class with polynomial Euler product and satisfies the functional equation $F(s)=H(s)\overline{F}(1-\bar{s})$. We prove that for every function of degree $1$ and for certain functions of degree $2$ belonging to the Selberg class, one has
  \begin{align*}
  \max_{T\leq t\leq 2T} \pm Z_F(t) &\gg \exp\left(C_F \sqrt{ \frac{{\log T}}{{\log \log T}} } \right),
  \end{align*}
  where the constant $C_F$ depends on $F(s)$ and are explicitly determined.

In this paper, we focus on the emergence of large densities of a cell population in finite time in the following parabolic-elliptic Keller-Segel system with signal-dependent motility and a source term of logistic type \begin{equation} \begin{cases} u_{\varepsilon t} = \Delta(e^{-v_\varepsilon}u_\varepsilon) + \varepsilon(\kappa u_\varepsilon - \mu u_\varepsilon^2) ,\\ 0 =\Delta v_{\varepsilon}-v_\varepsilon+ u_{\varepsilon}. \end{cases} \end{equation} in a smooth bounded domain $\Omega\subseteq\mathbb{R}^2$ with homogenuous Neumann boundary conditions and parameters $\kappa,\mu>0$ and $\varepsilon\in(0,1)$. We will show that the solution $u_\varepsilon$ can exceed any certain value $M>0$ before a time $T(M)>0$ under the condition that $\varepsilon>0$ is sufficiently small. To prove the result, we show convergence of classical solutions of the first system to classical solutions of the corresponding limit system as $\varepsilon\searrow 0$ \begin{equation}
  \begin{cases}
  u_t=\Delta(e^{-v}u),\\
  -\Delta v+v=u.
  \end{cases} \end{equation}
  The main challenge is to find suitable bounds for $u_\varepsilon$ and $v_\varepsilon$, that are $\varepsilon$-independent, in Lebesgue and H\"older spaces, whereas for fixed $\varepsilon>0$ global existence and boundedness is known.

We prove global well-posedness and scattering for the energy-critical defocusing nonlinear Schr\"odinger equation \begin{equation*} \begin{cases}
  i\partial_t u + \Delta u = |u|^{\frac{4}{d-2}} u,
  u(0) = u_0, \end{cases} \end{equation*} on hyperbolic space $\mathbb{H}^d(d \geq 4)$. We mainly utilize the concentration-compactness/rigidity method. We establish a linear profile decomposition on high-dimensional hyperbolic spaces and prove that the nonlinear profile on hyperbolic spaces can be approximated by the solutions of the energy-critical defocusing nonlinear Schr\"odinger equation on Euclidean spaces, then obtaining the global well-posedness and scattering of the energy-critical defocusing nonlinear Schr\"odinger equation on the high-dimensional hyperbolic space.

For a rotationally symmetric Ricci flow $g(t)=ds^2+f(s,t)^2g_{\mathbb S^{n-1}}$, $n\ge3$, we study the scale-invariant curvature anisotropy $A=1-f_s^2+ff_{ss}$ introduced by Angenent--Knopf. At the critical power $p_*=(n-1)/(n-2)$, with $\Phi=sgn(A)|A|^{p_*/2}$, we prove the distributional identity \[
  (\partial_t-\Delta)|A|^{p_*}
  =-\frac4{n-1}
  \left|\Phi_s+(n-1)\frac{f_s}{f}\Phi\right|^2. \] Integration against a conjugate heat kernel gives an anisotropic entropy formula. As an application, we obtain an entropy threshold for Type-I singularities on $\mathbb S^n$: if the entropy associated with a singular-time kernel is at most $1$ at an earlier regular time, every tangent flow at the corresponding singular point is a round shrinking sphere. This criterion follows from the $0/1$ quantization of the singular-time anisotropic density.

We investigate the fourth-order Jordan-Moore-Gibson-Thompson equation
  \begin{equation*}
  u_{tttt}+\al u_{ttt}+\bet u_{tt}-\gamma \Del u_{tt}-\del \Del u_{t}-\varrho \Del u=(f(u))_{ttt}+(f(u))_{tt}
  \end{equation*}
  under homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions in a smooth bounded domain $\Om \subset \R^n$ with $n\leq 3$, where
  $\al \in \R$ and $\bet,\gamma,\del,\varrho>0$. First, for any suitably regular initial data and assuming that $f\in C^4(\R)$ with $f(0)=0$, we prove that the corresponding initial-boundary value problem admits a unique maximal strong solution $u$ on $(0, \tm)$.
  Second, by combining three time-integrated formulations with a series of resolvent-regularized energy estimates, we show that gradient blow-up is ruled out in the sense that
  \begin{equation}\label{EE}
  \tm<\infty \quad\,\, \Rightarrow\quad\,\, \limsup_{t\nearrow \tm}\|u(\cd,t)\|_{L^{\infty}(\Om)}=\infty. \tag{$\star$}
  \end{equation} 
  Finally, assuming in addition that $f$ is convex and grows superlinearly in the sense that
  \begin{equation*}
  f''(\xi)\geq 0 \;\; \mbox{ for all}\;\;\xi \in\R, \qquad \frac{f(\xi)}{\xi}\rightarrow +\infty\;\;\mbox{ as}\;\; \xi\rightarrow +\infty , \qquad \int_{\xi_{0}}^{\infty}\frac{d\xi}{f(\xi)}<\infty \;\; \mbox{ for some}\;\; \xi_{0}>0,
  \end{equation*}
  we employ the extensibility criterion (\ref{EE}) to prove that, for a suitable class of initial data, the solution blows up in finite time in the $L^\infty$-norm. To the best of our knowledge, this provides the first rigorous description of finite-time blow-up for a genuinely nonlinear equation of fourth order in time.

We study the \emph{Polynomial Avoidance Problem}: given a finite collection $F \subseteq Z[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ of nonzero polynomials, find a vector $x \in Z^n$ of minimum $\ell_p$-norm such that $f(x)\neq0$ for every $f \in F$. Each polynomial is given sparsely as a list of its nonzero terms. The affine special case is the \emph{Hyperplane Avoidance Problem}. This special case arises in Barvinok-type algorithms for counting integer points in polyhedra, when certain rational generating functions must be evaluated at a removable singularity. Our main results are as follows:
  1) Let $D:=\sum_{f\in F} \text{deg} f$. Every instance admits a feasible vector $x$ satisfying
  \[
  \|x\|_1 \leq
  \min\left\{D,\frac{D+n}{2}\right\}.
  \]
  This bound is tight. Such a vector can be found by a deterministic algorithm whose bit complexity is polynomial in $D$ and the sparse encoding length of the input. Our proof uses a new algorithmic variant of the \emph{Combinatorial Nullstellensatz};
  2) In the affine case, with $m$ input polynomials, such a vector can be constructed by a fast deterministic polynomial-time algorithm using $O(nm)$ arithmetic operations. For the homogeneous case, Barvinok's original approach provides only the weaker guarantee $\|x\|_1 = O((nm)^{n-1})$;
  3) The problem is NP-hard for every $p\in R_{\geq 1}\cup\{\infty\}$, even for homogeneous linear forms;
  4) As an application, we improve the complexity bound for counting integer points in general rational polytopes. We also improve the bound for polyhedra of bounded codimension; this includes the counting version of the \emph{Unbounded Subset-Sum} problem as a special case.

Recently, Guo and Guo (Results Math $76:37, ~2021$) obtained curious $q$-analogues of the following two supercongruences conjectured by Guo and Schlosser \cite[Conjectures $12.6$ and $12.7$]{GuoSclssr ConstApprox 21}: \begin{align*} \sum_{n=0}^{(p-1)/3} (6n+1) \frac{\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)_n^{4}(2n)!}
  {(n!)^{4}\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)_{2n}} \equiv p \pmod{p^{3}} \qquad \text{for primes } p\equiv 1 \pmod{3}, \end{align*} \begin{align*} \sum_{n=0}^{(p+1)/3} (6n-1) \frac{\left(-\frac{1}{3}\right)_n^{4}(2n)!}
  {(n!)^{4}\left(-\frac{2}{3}\right)_{2n}} \equiv p \pmod{p^{3}} \qquad \text{for primes } p\equiv 2 \pmod{3}, \end{align*} where $(z)_n=z(z+1)\cdots(z+n-1)$ denotes the shifted factorial. In this paper, we establish a one-parameter extension of them. The main ingredients of our proofs are the creative microscoping method developed by Guo and Zudilin \cite{guo2019q} and Andrews' multi-series transformation.

The Characterization Problem asks whether a finite abelian group with Davenport constant at least four is determined, up to isomorphism, by its system of length sets. This is a central inverse problem in non-unique factorization theory: it asks whether the possible numbers of irreducible factors retain enough information to recover the underlying class group. For rings of integers of algebraic number fields, an affirmative answer means that the ideal class group can be recovered from these factorization lengths alone. We resolve this problem for all homocyclic groups $C_n^r$: if $G$ is a finite abelian group with $\mathsf D(G)\ge4$ and $\mathcal L(G)=\mathcal L(C_n^r)$, then $G\cong C_n^r$. The cross number of a sequence is the sum of the reciprocals of the orders of its terms. This weighted measure of sequence length links zero-sum theory with half-factoriality, and determining its maximal value for minimal zero-sum sequences is a longstanding extremal problem. We also obtain an exact formula for the maximal cross number of minimal zero-sum sequences over subsets whose minimum distance is divisible by the rank of the ambient group minus one. For $G\cong C_{n_1}\oplus\cdots\oplus C_{n_r}$ with $1<n_1\mid\cdots\mid n_r$ and $r\ge\max\{2,n_r-2\}$, we prove \[
  \mathsf K(G,r-1)=1+(r-1)\sum_{p\mid n_1}
  \left(1-p^{-\mathsf v_p(n_1)}\right), \] which is a key ingredient in resolving the Characterization Problem for homocyclic groups.

We prove the (signed) Grothendieck join--meet positivity $$ G_{\lambda\vee\mu}G_{\lambda\wedge\mu}
  -G_\lambda G_\mu \in \sum_{
u} \mathbb{N}\cdot (-1)^{|
u|-|\lambda|-|\mu|}G_
u $$ for stable Grothendieck polynomials using geometric Satake equivalence, thereby resolving a conjecture posed by Thomas and Yong.

This paper establishes uniform local large-deviation asymptotics for excursions of a L\'evy process $X$ with negative drift $-\beta$ and $\alpha$-locally regularly varying L\'evy measure $\nu$, reflected at its running infimum. Under the excursion measure $\underline n$, we first prove that the lifetime $\zeta$ and height $\overline\epsilon$ satisfy \begin{equation*}
  \underline{n}\big(\zeta\in(t,t+\delta] \big) \sim \underline{n}(\overline\epsilon\in\big (\beta t,\beta t+\beta\delta]\big)
  \sim\underline{n}(\zeta)\cdot\nu\big((\beta t,\beta t+\beta \delta]\big),
  \quad \mbox{as } t\to\infty, \end{equation*} uniformly in $\delta\in[\delta_0,\infty]$ for every $\delta_0>0$. In contrast to the asymptotic equivalence of the corresponding tail events, fixed-width lifetime and height windows have asymptotically negligible relative overlap. Nevertheless, for any positive function $\theta$ satisfying $|X_t+\beta t|/\theta_t\to0$ in probability, \begin{equation*}
  \underline n\big( |\overline\epsilon-\beta t| \leq \theta_t \,\big|\, \zeta\in (t,t+\delta] \big) \sim
  \underline n\big( |\zeta-t| \leq \theta_t \,\big|\, \overline\epsilon \in (\beta t,\beta t+\beta\delta] \big) \to 1 . \end{equation*} For conditioning windows of sublinear width bounded away from zero, both conditional excursion laws converge under spatial rescaling to a single-jump process with deterministic jump size and random arrival time determined by the integrated excursion survival function. An additional time rescaling yields the same deterministic linear descent killed at zero. Finally, we identify the joint limiting distribution of the unscaled pre-terminal position and terminal overshoot, including continuous and discontinuous termination.

We study the minimax regret rate for episodic kernel-based reinforcement learning, where the expected reward and transition density belong to a Sobolev reproducing kernel Hilbert space, with continuous state and action spaces, and adversarial initial states. For Sobolev smoothness parameter $
u>0$, state dimension $d_S$, and action dimension $d_A$, we prove that the minimax expected regret over $T$ episodes is $\widetilde\Theta(T^{\max\{e_A,e_B\}})$, where $e_A= rac{
u+d_A}{2
u+d_A}$, $e_B= rac{
u+d_A+3d_S/2}{2
u+d_A+2d_S}$ with the other model parameters fixed. The two exponents reflect the distinct difficulties of action selection and learning across states. We introduce Sobolev Optimal Value Iteration (SOVI), which attains this rate from observed rewards and transitions. For fixed smoothness and reference-kernel lengthscale, its regret bound has a prefactor polynomial in the state-action dimension $d=d_S+d_A$, horizon $H$, transition and reward RKHS norm bounds $u,B_r$, and kernel norm-comparison factor $\Lambda$, up to logarithmic factors. The algorithm separates initial action selection from continuation-value estimation and combines local polynomial approximation with the shared RKHS structure of the approximation errors. Our matching upper and lower bounds determine the optimal dependence on the number of episodes for Sobolev kernel-based reinforcement learning. Thus, we partially address the open problem in Vakili(2024) under the condition of the Sobolev kernel setup.

Let $\pi(H)$ be the Tur\'an density of an r-uniform hypergraph $H$ and let $H_k^r$ denote the $r$-uniform hypergraph on $r+1$ vertices with exactly $k$ edges, where $1\le k\le r+1$. Sidorenko~(JCT-B, 2024) proved that $\pi(H_3^r)\ge (1.7215-o(1))r^{-2}$ as $r\to\infty$ and $\pi(H_k^r)\ge (C_k+o(1))r^{-(1+1/(k-2))}$ for fixed $k$ as $r\to\infty$. Clemen~later improved the first bound to $\pi(H_3^r)\ge cr^{-2}\sqrt{\log r}$ for some constant $c>0$.
  In this article, we prove the following results. \begin{itemize} \item For any fixed $\varepsilon>0$, there is a constant $c_\varepsilon>0$ such that $$\pi(H_3^r)\ge \frac{c_\varepsilon}{r(\log r)^{2+\varepsilon}}.$$ %$\pi(H_3^r)\ge 1/(r(\log r)^{2+o(1)})$. Together with the known upper bound $\pi(H_3^r)\le1/r$, this implies $\pi(H_3^r)=r^{-1+o(1)}$.
  \item For every $3\le k\le r+1$, let $s=\min\{k-2,r-k+2\}$. Then \begin{equation*}
  0\le \frac{k-2}{r}-\pi(H_k^r)
  \le \frac{128}{r}\left(\sqrt{s\log\frac{er}{s}}+\log\frac{er}{s}\right). \end{equation*} This estimate yields several asymptotically sharp results for $\pi(H_k^r)$. For example, $\pi(H_k^r)=(1+o(1))(k-2)/r$ when $\log(er/(k))=o(k)$.
  \end{itemize}

We extend the simultaneous popular polynomial difference theorem of Conlon, Dong, and the author to polynomial shifts along prescribed directions in $\mathbb{F}_p^D$. Let $P_1,\ldots,P_k\in\mathbb{Z}[t]$ be linearly independent polynomials with zero constant terms, and let $v_1,\ldots,v_k\in\mathbb{Z}^D\setminus\{0\}$. We show that, for every $\varepsilon>0$, every sufficiently large prime $p$, and every $A\subseteq\mathbb{F}_p^D$ of density $\alpha$, there exists a nonzero $d\in\mathbb{F}_p$ such that \[
  \mathbb{E}_{\underline{x}\in\mathbb{F}_p^D}
  \mathbf{1}_A(\underline{x})
  \prod_{\substack{1\leq i\leq k\\ \omega_i=1}}
  \mathbf{1}_A\bigl(\underline{x}+P_i(d)v_i\bigr)
  \geq \alpha^{\,1+\sum_{i=1}^k\omega_i}-\varepsilon \] simultaneously for every $\omega\in\{0,1\}^k$. The main new ingredient is a directional arithmetic regularity decomposition adapted to mixed directional box norms.

Let $\Delta_{\rm d}$ be the nonnegative discrete Laplacian on $\mathbb Z^d$. We prove that, for $0<p\le1$, \[
  \|\Delta_{\rm d}^{i\gamma}\|_{H^p_{\Delta_{\rm d}}\to H^p_{\Delta_{\rm d}}}
  \simeq (1+|\gamma|)^{d(1/p-1/2)}, \] and, for $1<p<\infty$, \[
  \|\Delta_{\rm d}^{i\gamma}\|_{\ell^p\to\ell^p}
  \simeq (1+|\gamma|)^{d|1/p-1/2|}. \] The estimates are sharp with respect to the exponent of $1+|\gamma|$.
  We also explain why the general spectral multiplier theorem and the usual Calder\'on--Zygmund approach do not give the sharp power.

Let $X$ be a complex klt Fano variety. We study the rational total index $\tau_X$ and the nef complexity $c_X=\dim X+\rho(X)-\tau_X$. Previous joint work of the author with Enwright, Filipazzi, Gongyo, Moraga, Svaldi, and Wang established the corresponding nonnegativity and zero-case characterization in the broader framework of a special class of generalized log Calabi--Yau pairs. Here we give a direct proof in the Fano setting, prove that there is no value strictly between zero and one, and classify all klt Fano varieties with $c_X<2$, without assuming $\mathbb Q$-factoriality. At complexity one, after splitting off projective-space factors, the remaining factor is a normal quadric of Picard number one, a linear blow-up of projective space, or an integral $(1,1)$ hypersurface in a product of projective spaces. If $1<c_X<2$, only two weighted-projective families occur; in particular, no smooth Fano variety has nef complexity strictly between one and two. We also determine the spectrum when the dimension is allowed to vary: \[
  \{0\}\cup\left\{2-\frac2q \ \middle| \ q\in\mathbb Z_{\geq2}\right\}
  \cup(\mathbb Q\cap[2,\infty)). \] Most proofs in this paper were developed from arguments generated by OpenAI's ChatGPT through discussions initiated and guided by the author; see the statement on the use of generative AI.

Let $p_d=(d^2+1)/(d(d-1))$ and $q_d=(d^2+1)/(d-1)$. We prove that the local spherical maximal operator $\sup_{1\leq t\leq 2}|f*d\sigma_t|$ is bounded from $L^{p_d}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ to $L^{q_d}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ for every $d\geq 2$. Previously only the restricted weak type estimate was known at this endpoint. Together with known results, this completes the characterization of the strong type $L^p\to L^q$ bounds for the local spherical maximal operator. We also prove the maximal estimate \[
  \Big\|\sup_{t\in\mathbb{R}}|e^{it\sqrt{-\Delta}}f|\Big\|_{L^{2(d+1)/(d-1)}(\mathbb{R}^d)}
  \lesssim\|f\|_{\dot H^{d/(d+1)}(\mathbb{R}^d)} \] for the wave equation at the critical exponent. Both results are obtained by exploiting the gain in bilinear estimates that comes from the interaction between high and low frequencies.

We characterize the following three classes of ordered trees:
  $\qquad$ (1) trees $T$ satisfying $R_<(T,K_n)=O(n)$;
  $\qquad$ (2) $\chi$-bounding trees;
  $\qquad$ (3) Ramsey-good trees.
  In particular, we show that the first two classes coincide. The second characterization answers a question of Axenovich, Rollin and Ueckerdt, and the third resolves a problem of Balko and Poljak.
  In our proof, we also introduce several novel constructions of ordered graphs with sublinear independence number, giving lower bounds on $R_<(F,K_n)$ for various ordered forests $F$.

We investigate the qualitative properties of positive classical solutions $u$ to critical Hartree equations on punctured domains, assuming that $u(x)\to+\infty$ as $x\to0$. For the global problem set in $\mathbb R^n\setminus\{0\}$, we prove that such solutions are radial and nonincreasing in $|x|$. For the local problem in $B_R\setminus\{0\}$, we establish the upper bound $u(x)\leqslant C|x|^{-(n-2)/2}$ near the origin and the asymptotic radial symmetry estimate
  \begin{equation*}
  u(x)=\bar u(|x|)\bigl(1+\mathcal O(|x|)\bigr)\qquad\text{as }x\to0,
  \end{equation*}
  where $\bar u$ is the spherical average of $u$. Our approach combines integral representations, blow-up analysis, and the method of moving spheres. A main difficulty is controlling the nonlocal Hartree potential under Kelvin inversion and in the blow-up limit. We also give an alternative proof of the classification of positive entire classical solutions in $\mathbb{R}^{n}$, which is used to identify the blow-up profile.

We study the Neumann boundary value problem $$ -\operatorname{div}\left( rac{
abla u}{\sqrt{1-|
abla u|^2}}ight) = \lambda a(x)g(u) \quad 	ext{in } \Omega, \qquad  rac{
abla u}{\sqrt{1-|
abla u|^2}}\cdot \mathbf n = 0 \quad 	ext{on } \partial\Omega, $$ where $\Omega \subset \mathbb R^N$ is a bounded convex domain, $a\in L^\infty(\Omega)$ is an indefinite weight with $\int_\Omega a(x) \, dx< 0$, and $g$ is a nonlinearity. Under suitable assumptions on $g$, we prove the existence of two positive solutions for sufficiently large $\lambda>0$, using Szulkin's theory for nonsmooth functionals: a global minimizer $u_\lambda^{(l)}$ with negative energy, and a mountain-pass critical point $u_\lambda^{(s)}$ with positive energy. Furthermore, we study the asymptotic behaviour as $\lambda	o+\infty$ of both families of solutions. For the ground states we prove that, up to a subsequence, $u_\lambda^{(l)}	o u_\infty$ uniformly, where $u_\infty$ satisfies $\|
abla u_\infty\|_{L^\infty(\Omega)}=1$ and is characterized by being a maximizer of $\int_\Omega a(x) G(u) \, dx$ and a minimizer of the kinetic energy among all such maximizers. In the model case $g(u)=|u|^{p-2}u$, $p>2$, we also show that the mountain-pass energy level satisfies the upper bound $c_\lambda=O(\lambda^{-2/(p-2)})$, and that $u_\lambda^{(s)}	o0$ in $C(\overline\Omega)$.

For every $x\ge 0$, let $R^{x}=\{ R^{x}_{t}\} _{t\ge 0}$ be a three-dimensional Bessel process started at $x$. Given $x>0$, with the aid of Pitman's theorem, a path transformation $T_{x}$ under which the process $T_{x}(R^{y})$ is distributed as $R^{x}$ for any $y>0$ is constructed. In fact, we prove that, for every $x,y>0$, the two three-dimensional processes \begin{align*}
  \bigl( R^{x},T_{y}(R^{x}),\mathcal{P}(-R^{x})+x\bigr) && \text{and} &&
  \bigl( T_{x}(R^{y}),R^{y},\mathcal{P}(-R^{y})+y\bigr) \end{align*} are identical in law, where $\mathcal{P}$ is Pitman's transformation. In the course of our exploration, it will be revealed that, for any $x>0$, the process $\mathcal{P}(-R^{x})+x$ has the same law as the standard three-dimensional Bessel process $R^{0}$, which is then applied to derive a disintegration formula for the law of $R^{x}$ in terms of Pitman's transformation. An application of the formula to three-dimensional Bessel bridges is also presented.

Let $P$ be a trigonometric polynomial with only real zeros. We prove that at most $\frac{2}{3}$ of its zeros coincide with the zeros of the Riemann zeta function $\zeta(s)$ on the critical line. We also show that \begin{equation*}
  \sup_{\substack{T\leq x\leq2T\\ P(x)=0}}
  \log \left|\zeta\left(\frac{1}{2}+ix\right)\right|
  \geq
  (1+o(1))\sqrt{\frac{\log T}{6\log\log T}}. \end{equation*} These results extend the corresponding theorems of Li and Radziwill on the zeros of $\zeta(s)$ along vertical arithmetic progressions. The main novelty in our approach is the use of the work of Kurasov and Sarnak and Lee-Yang representations of Alon-Cohen-Vinzant, which show that the corresponding measure $\sum_{P(\gamma)=0} {\bf \delta}_\gamma$ is crystalline.

We study the existence and qualitative properties of non-negative solutions to singular 1-Laplacian problems of the form \begin{equation*}
  \begin{cases}
  -\Delta_1 u = g(u)|D u| + h(u)f & \text{in } \Omega,\newline
  u=0 & \text{on } \partial\Omega,
  \end{cases} \end{equation*} where $\Omega\subset \mathbb{R}^N$ is a bounded domain with Lipschitz boundary, $0<f\in L^{N,\infty}(\Omega)$, and $g,h$ are continuous positive functions that may blow up at the origin. We establish the existence of a positive bounded solution even when $g$ is non-integrable at zero and $h(0)=+\infty$, breaking the non-existence phenomenon appearing in the associated $p$-Laplacian problems. Furthermore, we prove that solutions possess a non-zero boundary trace on a set of positive measure of $\partial\Omega$ whenever $g(s)$ grows sufficiently fast near $s=0$; by contrast, when this growth condition fails, solutions vanishing on the boundary may appear. Finally, when $h(0)<+\infty$, we provide a sharp threshold classification of trivial and non-trivial solutions depending on the size of $f$.
